answer.worked:
If the prize requires $n+1$ wins, the maximum number of games played is $2n+1$ (if both win $n$ games, the next game decides it). By symmetry, since both players are equally likely to win any game, the overall probability that player $A$ gets the prize eventually is $\frac{1}{2}$.
Player $A$ can win in $k$ games, where $k$ ranges from $n+1$ to $2n+1$.
The probability $A$ wins in exactly $k$ games is the probability $A$ wins $n$ of the first $k-1$ games, and then wins the $k$-th game:
$$\begin{align*}
P(\text{wins in } k) &= \binom{k-1}{n}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{k-1} \times \frac{1}{2} \\
&= \binom{k-1}{n}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^k
\end{align*}$$
Summing over all possible winning lengths $k$:
$$\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=n+1}^{2n+1} \binom{k-1}{n}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^k &= \frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}$$
Let $r = k-1$. Then $r$ ranges from $n$ to $2n$:
$$\begin{align*}
\sum_{r=n}^{2n} \binom{r}{n}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{r+1} &= \frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}$$
Multiply both sides by $2^{2n+1}$:
$$\begin{align*}
\sum_{r=n}^{2n} \binom{r}{n} 2^{2n+1-(r+1)} &= \frac{1}{2} \times 2^{2n+1} \\
\sum_{r=n}^{2n} \binom{r}{n} 2^{2n-r} &= 2^{2n}
\end{align*}$$
Expanding the summation:
$$\begin{align*}
\binom{n}{n}2^{n} + \binom{n+1}{n}2^{n-1} + \binom{n+2}{n}2^{n-2} + \dots + \binom{2n}{n}2^{0} &= 2^{2n}
\end{align*}$$
Which is the required result.

